00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:10.910
registry, and then some of
the social features-- again,

00:03:10.910 --> 00:03:13.937
the gifting and friend lists.

00:03:13.937 --> 00:03:15.520
YURI GRINSHTEYN: All
right, thank you.

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:18.040
So one of the things you
just mentioned were services.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:19.750
So tell me about
how do you monitor

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:21.953
the health of these services?

00:03:21.953 --> 00:03:23.370
MICHAEL DEBRECENI:
When I started,

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:26.100
we were using a traditional
monitoring approach.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.482
So a combination of Prometheus
and Google Cloud Monitoring.

00:03:29.482 --> 00:03:31.940
We actually like the way that
the two worked together quite

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:35.510
nicely, and so we support both.

00:03:35.510 --> 00:03:37.850
The traditional
monitoring approach

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.560
means that you pick
a set of metrics

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:42.320
that you think represent how
well the system is working.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.120
So this will be a mix of system
metrics like memory and CPU use

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:48.890
and then game-level
metrics that might

00:03:48.890 --> 00:03:51.530
be things like how many
Pokemon are being captured.

00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:53.330
How many spells are being cast?

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:55.440
How many players are logged in?

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:57.045
You then set
thresholds for normal

00:03:57.045 --> 00:03:58.670
and then watch for
things that indicate

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:00.630
something unusual is going on.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:03.350
So maybe we see that the number
of players has dropped off

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:05.380
or the number of
successful Pokemon copters

00:04:05.380 --> 00:04:07.600
has suddenly dropped off.

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:10.380
And this works fairly well.

00:04:10.380 --> 00:04:12.470
There are some limitations
to this approach.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.990
So number one, it tells you
how the servers are doing,

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:17.959
but it doesn't necessarily tell
you how the players are doing.

00:04:17.959 --> 00:04:21.709
There's some guesswork involved
in picking the correct metrics

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:23.990
and figuring out
which metrics matter.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:26.240
Also picking appropriate
thresholds so that you end up

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:29.120
with alerts that are
sensitive, but not so sensitive

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.620
that they're to the
point of being noisy.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:35.260
It's very, very easy to
focus on wrong metrics,

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:36.940
and it's very easy
to miss problems

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.300
that may involve a combination
of metrics that combine

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.435
in ways you never anticipated.

00:04:42.435 --> 00:04:43.810
One of the things
that we learned

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:48.700
is that distributed systems tend
to fail in very complex ways.

00:04:48.700 --> 00:04:52.540
With Wizards Unite, we piloted
a SLO monitoring approach

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:54.940
with the help of our
partners at Google

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:57.770
to complement our
traditional monitoring.

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:01.125
So the approach here
is to look at the game

00:05:01.125 --> 00:05:02.500
from the point of
view of players

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:04.150
rather than the servers.

00:05:04.150 --> 00:05:07.000
So we measure things
like request latency,

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.610
the success rate for requests
from players, both overall,

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:14.380
and then a more detailed level,
such as the individual actions

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.020
that they may
perform in the game.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.320
We then set performance
targets for these metrics,

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.190
and end up with service
level objectives, or SLOs.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.020
We can then I'll
subtract error budgets

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:28.390
based on how well we
stick with these SLOs

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:31.030
and how well we comply
over the long term.

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:33.670
We then alert based
on absolute violations

00:05:33.670 --> 00:05:37.992
or if we start burning
error budget excessively.

00:05:37.992 --> 00:05:39.200
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Makes sense.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:41.075
So it sounds like some
of the primary reasons

00:05:41.075 --> 00:05:44.260
you're adopting SLOs is to
really focus on user experience

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:48.100
and how users are
perceiving the game.

00:05:48.100 --> 00:05:49.510
Was that the primary motivation?

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:51.190
And then what other
benefits did you

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:52.712
see from making this change?

00:05:52.712 --> 00:05:54.920
MICHAEL DEBRECENI: So there
are a couple of benefits.

00:05:54.920 --> 00:06:00.308
So as we realized, players
are not really interested

00:06:00.308 --> 00:06:01.600
with the details of our server.

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:03.550
They do care if the
game is working, though,

00:06:03.550 --> 00:06:06.010
so we should measure that.

00:06:06.010 --> 00:06:08.740
So what we found is
that by implementing

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.520
a couple of very simple SLOs
based off of the metrics

00:06:12.520 --> 00:06:15.190
from our load balancer,
latency and success rate,

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:18.962
we're able to tell a lot
about the player experience.

00:06:18.962 --> 00:06:20.920
They also keep us very
honest because we're not

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.750
depending on the servers to
report their own metrics.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:26.625
One of the things, obviously,
is that if a server crashes,

00:06:26.625 --> 00:06:28.000
it's not going to
report anything

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:29.260
that says it's in trouble.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:31.670
Something else has to do that.

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:34.540
So the effect of this is that
problems have nowhere to hide.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:36.290
If there's something
going on whether it's

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:37.820
something internal
within the server

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.400
or something with our
network infrastructure,

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.620
we're going to see it
as an SLO violation

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:46.710
if it affects the players, and
then we'll receive an alert.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.260
So some of the benefits.

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:51.780
We tend to see better
quality alerts.

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:55.790
We have much less alert noise
with our SLO violation alerts.

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:57.380
We do know that when
we get an alert,

00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:59.005
we know that there's
something going on

00:06:59.005 --> 00:07:00.590
that we need to address.

00:07:00.590 --> 00:07:03.050
The alert doesn't necessarily
tell us what the problem is,

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:05.080
but it does let us know
that we need to start

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:06.332
our troubleshooting process.

00:07:06.332 --> 00:07:08.540
And this is where we may
look at some of our existing

00:07:08.540 --> 00:07:11.670
traditional monitoring.

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:14.000
It's also actually
taught us a couple

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.230
of things about our
deployments that we've done

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:21.450
and some of the regressions
that may come up afterwards.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:25.070
So we found that by watching
outside of the servers,

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:26.900
we can now see the
effect of doing

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:30.500
even things like a deployment
or planned maintenance.

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.230
This means that we've
actually changed the way

00:07:33.230 --> 00:07:35.150
that we do some of
our deployments based

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:36.770
on some of the
observed disruption

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:39.110
that we were causing.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:41.090
The other thing
that we've noticed

00:07:41.090 --> 00:07:44.600
is that we can watch for what's
called a slow burn alert, where

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:47.120
we're very slowly
consuming error budget,

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:49.170
but faster than we should be.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:51.980
This would be something like
a slow leak in a car tire

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.807
where you may not notice that
there is a problem from day

00:07:54.807 --> 00:07:56.390
to day, but you will
definitely notice

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:58.250
when you have a flat tire.

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:01.490
So what this is, it's
watching the cumulative effect

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:04.520
of small SLO violations
that may start

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:06.470
after we've made some change.

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:09.290
This would be lost in the noise
of a traditional monitoring

00:08:09.290 --> 00:08:10.070
approach.

00:08:10.070 --> 00:08:14.450
But with our slow monitoring
and our error budget tracking,

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:15.590
they're now very visible.

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:17.090
And now we know,
again, that we need

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:18.727
to start looking
for a regression.

00:08:18.727 --> 00:08:20.310
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So
this sounds great.

00:08:20.310 --> 00:08:22.268
Now you have a better
sense of user experience.

00:08:22.268 --> 00:08:24.002
Your alerts are
of higher quality.

00:08:24.002 --> 00:08:25.460
When you get an
alert, you actually

00:08:25.460 --> 00:08:27.020
know that there's a problem.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:30.260
You can also catch problems
like the slow burn situation

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:31.120
that you described.

00:08:31.120 --> 00:08:33.054
So this all sounds great.

00:08:33.054 --> 00:08:35.429
What's been the hardest part
about making this transition

00:08:35.429 --> 00:08:37.610
and making this change?

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:39.860
MICHAEL DEBRECENI: I think
selling the idea internally

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:42.230
was actually one of
the harder things.

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:44.840
A lot of people understand
what traditional monitoring is,

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:47.990
but they don't understand
necessarily why we also

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:49.530
want to do SLO monitoring.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:52.520
Why isn't traditional
monitoring good enough?

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:54.430
So I had to explain that
this is a complement

00:08:54.430 --> 00:08:55.630
to traditional monitoring.

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:58.260
We're not replacing our
existing monitoring;

00:08:58.260 --> 00:08:59.260
we're just adding to it.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:01.390
We're doing something
that means that we're

00:09:01.390 --> 00:09:03.850
looking at the same metric
data, but in a different way

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:06.050
and interpreting it differently.

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:08.200
One of the benefits
that we realized

00:09:08.200 --> 00:09:13.420
is, again, detecting problems
that we didn't even see coming.

00:09:13.420 --> 00:09:15.970
The other obstacle
that I ran into

00:09:15.970 --> 00:09:20.830
is the conception that
SLOs are just for SREs,

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:23.590
and nobody else needs
to pay any attention.

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:25.640
And actually this
is not the case.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.930
We definitely need and want
input from product and the rest

00:09:28.930 --> 00:09:30.400
of the business.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:33.640
As SREs, we're very good
at measuring things.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:37.210
Product is very good at knowing
what we need to be measuring

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:39.430
and what we need
to be looking for.

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:41.560
Unfortunately, the product
team is also very busy,

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:44.440
and unless they
understand their role

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:48.820
in coming up with good SLOs,
it's hard to get their time.

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:50.830
So the approach we
took was to make

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:54.040
this as easy as possible
by focusing on one or two

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:56.890
starter SLOs where we
had a pretty good idea

00:09:56.890 --> 00:09:59.470
and just needed
confirmation from product.

00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:02.380
Once we had that and could
demonstrate some value,

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:05.830
we were then able to
bring in product and work

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:07.095
on some more advanced SLOs.

00:10:09.402 --> 00:10:11.860
YURI GRINSHTEYN: So selling
this internally is a challenge.

00:10:11.860 --> 00:10:13.703
Getting product to buy
into is a challenge.

00:10:13.703 --> 00:10:15.620
So that's something that
makes a lot of sense.

00:10:15.620 --> 00:10:17.800
I totally get that.

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:19.600
Looking back on your
journey, would you

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:20.892
have done anything differently?

00:10:20.892 --> 00:10:22.850
Or what would you
do differently?

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:25.433
MICHAEL DEBRECENI: So one of the
things that I would have done

00:10:25.433 --> 00:10:28.870
is to start simple, demonstrate
value quickly, and focus

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:31.390
on the quick wins
before taking on some

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:33.860
of the more advanced use cases.

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:36.940
So as an example, one of
the things we tried to do

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:41.530
was set up SLOs based off of the
custom metrics in Prometheus.

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:44.710
This involves setting up a
sidecar, which ingests metrics,

00:10:44.710 --> 00:10:46.510
and to Stackdriver.

00:10:46.510 --> 00:10:49.960
We had some concerns about the
cardinality of that metric,

00:10:49.960 --> 00:10:51.910
of the metrics
that we were using,

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:53.680
and wanted to
pre-summarize the data

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:57.610
before sending it in to the
cloud monitoring service.

00:10:57.610 --> 00:11:01.060
We found that that was actually,
at the time, fairly difficult,

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:06.370
because the sidecar was in a
fairly early development state

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:08.600
compared to where it is now.

00:11:08.600 --> 00:11:12.430
So we ended up working
with the development

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:14.680
team behind the sidecar
and were eventually

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:17.170
able to add the
feature that supports

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:18.910
aggregated metrics coming in.

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:20.170
This was a huge help for us.

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:21.830
It let us do what we want.

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:24.380
And the sidecar is now
in a much better state.

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:26.890
However, it did take a
long time to get something.

00:11:26.890 --> 00:11:29.170
Looking back, I would
have just started

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:30.640
with the load balancer metrics.

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:32.650
They actually gave us
something useful very

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:35.110
quickly in about an
afternoon's work.

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:37.990
And that gave us about 90%
of what we were looking for.

00:11:37.990 --> 00:11:41.285
They were also much,
much simpler to set up.

00:11:41.285 --> 00:11:42.910
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Yeah, I can understand

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:45.340
that, just using the out-of-box
load balancer metrics,

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:47.350
rather than kind of building
it along with Prometheus

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:49.433
and then also maintaining
that additional side car

00:11:49.433 --> 00:11:50.322
infrastructure.

00:11:50.322 --> 00:11:52.280
Yeah, would certainly
make things a lot easier.

00:11:52.280 --> 00:11:53.947
So that's an excellent
lesson to pass on

00:11:53.947 --> 00:11:56.780
to the folks that are looking
to start their journey down

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:59.860
this path as well.

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:01.360
Other than that,
what else would you

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:04.880
advise someone who is looking
to follow in your footsteps

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:06.445
and reproduce this?

00:12:06.445 --> 00:12:08.570
MICHAEL DEBRECENI: So I
would say, don't be afraid.

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:12.430
This is actually not
that hard to set up.

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:15.010
Like I said, with the
load balancer metrics,

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:16.570
we had something
in an afternoon.

00:12:16.570 --> 00:12:18.820
It was very straightforward.

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:21.520
The other thing I would say
is that if you can measure it,

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:23.320
you can turn it into an SLO.

00:12:23.320 --> 00:12:26.260
So think about the existing
metrics that you already have

00:12:26.260 --> 00:12:28.360
and see if there
are ways that you

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:32.080
can turn them into an SLO that
describes user experience.

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:34.750
Obviously there are some
that will work very well.

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:38.175
In our case, the load balancer
metrics were very helpful.

00:12:38.175 --> 00:12:38.800
They were free.

00:12:38.800 --> 00:12:41.740
They were available
out of the box.

00:12:41.740 --> 00:12:43.490
Don't forget, also,
to start simple and go

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:45.650
with a quick and easy
win before taking

00:12:45.650 --> 00:12:47.600
on the more advanced
challenges that may

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:51.110
need some buy-in from others.

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:53.260
Finally, pick the right tools.

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:55.660
Pick a tool that's flexible
and that works well

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:57.580
with your existing environment.

00:12:57.580 --> 00:13:02.540
In our case, we chose the cloud
monitoring SLO API from Google.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:05.410
This supports creating
many types of SLOs,

00:13:05.410 --> 00:13:07.840
works on all cloud
platforms, and supports

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:10.300
containerized and virtual
machine environments.

00:13:10.300 --> 00:13:13.608
We're very happy with how
it's worked out for us.

00:13:13.608 --> 00:13:15.150
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
Thank you very much.

00:13:15.150 --> 00:13:15.830
I appreciate that.

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:17.205
And again, thanks
for being here.

00:13:17.205 --> 00:13:19.730
I think this is some
super useful lessons

00:13:19.730 --> 00:13:22.460
that I think you have learned
that I'm very happy that you're

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:24.600
able to pass on to others.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.720
So thank you very much, again.

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:28.190
From here, what
I'd love to do is

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:30.970
to walk through some practical
examples of how folks

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:33.500
are watching this session
and take some of the lessons

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:36.500
that Mike has learned and apply
them to your own environment.

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:40.320
First, I want to show you how to
do exactly what Mike is saying.

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:43.340
How do you create some of
those basic availability

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:46.550
and latency SLOs using some
of the out of the box features

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:48.223
in Google Cloud Monitoring?

00:13:48.223 --> 00:13:50.390
They're there for you to
use without really the need

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:53.600
to do any additional
application instrumentation.

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:55.910
The way this works is
that you essentially

00:13:55.910 --> 00:14:00.410
have to start by
defining your service.

00:14:00.410 --> 00:14:02.870
The basic premise here is
that you have a service,

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:04.550
and you can be
measuring service health

00:14:04.550 --> 00:14:08.990
using service-level
objectives for that service.

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:11.780
There are two primary ways
by which you can define

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:14.360
the service using the UI.

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:17.030
If your service is running
on Google Kubernetes Engine

00:14:17.030 --> 00:14:20.570
or GKE, the UI will
actually let you

00:14:20.570 --> 00:14:24.650
pick one of the GKE primitives
to essentially create

00:14:24.650 --> 00:14:28.060
this logical construct which
you can use as a service.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:30.080
So you can use things
like GKE services,

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:33.620
GKE deployments, even
something like a GKE namespace,

00:14:33.620 --> 00:14:37.070
and tie your service
definition to that.

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:39.470
If your GKE cluster
has Istio installed

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:42.620
on it, which is an open
source service mesh that

00:14:42.620 --> 00:14:45.620
allows you to do things like
traffic routing, security

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:49.520
encryption, and telemetry
or observability,

00:14:49.520 --> 00:14:52.040
then its services running
within the Istio service mesh

00:14:52.040 --> 00:14:54.020
will also be exposed
for you here,

00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:55.870
and you can simply
pick one of those.

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:59.510
That's the first step, to select
a service that's already been

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:02.750
defined or define it yourself.

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:04.640
If you're not running
on GKE, if you actually

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:07.160
want to define just
a custom service,

00:15:07.160 --> 00:15:10.782
there's an API available that
you can use to do that as well.

00:15:10.782 --> 00:15:12.740
So that's the first step
is, select the service

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:14.390
or define the service.

00:15:14.390 --> 00:15:16.790
The next step would
be to pick the SLI.

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:18.658
SLI stands for service
level indicator,

00:15:18.658 --> 00:15:20.450
and this is the metric
that you're actually

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:24.560
going to use to essentially
determine, at any given point,

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:26.090
is your service healthy or not.

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:27.850
This is the thing
you're measuring,

00:15:27.850 --> 00:15:31.210
that you can be using as a
proxy for user happiness.

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:34.130
Are my users happy,
or are they not?

00:15:34.130 --> 00:15:37.325
So specifically
for Istio services,

00:15:37.325 --> 00:15:38.950
the SLO monitoring
scheme will actually

00:15:38.950 --> 00:15:43.360
allow you to pick availability
or latency right there,

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:45.440
because it's aware that--

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:47.770
it's aware of what
an Istio service is.

00:15:47.770 --> 00:15:51.010
And it knows the Istio metrics
they use to expose things

00:15:51.010 --> 00:15:53.070
like availability and latency.

00:15:53.070 --> 00:15:56.230
And you just need to just pick
the kind that you want to use.

00:15:56.230 --> 00:15:58.630
If this is not an
Istio service, you

00:15:58.630 --> 00:16:01.060
can actually select Other
and essentially pick

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:04.000
any metric within
Google cloud monitoring

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:08.200
to be used as the SLI, or
service level indicator,

00:16:08.200 --> 00:16:10.870
for that particular service.

00:16:10.870 --> 00:16:13.360
And the last step is to actually
define the service level

00:16:13.360 --> 00:16:14.500
objective.

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:17.150
So you may recall
that the service level

00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:19.390
the indicator is the metric
that we're going to use

00:16:19.390 --> 00:16:21.250
to measure service health.

00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:23.640
The service level
objective is the target

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:25.390
that we're going to
put that will actually

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:28.840
tell us whether our
service is healthy enough.

00:16:28.840 --> 00:16:30.730
So that's the last
step of the process,

00:16:30.730 --> 00:16:32.770
is you pick and set your SLO.

00:16:32.770 --> 00:16:34.960
You determine, what is
the level of reliability

00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:38.200
that this particular
service needs to deliver.

00:16:38.200 --> 00:16:40.450
You specify the time
window over which

00:16:40.450 --> 00:16:41.960
you're going to measure it.

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:44.830
You have the option of using
a calendar window or a rolling

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:47.450
window and the length
of that window.

00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:50.740
It's then going to show you what
is your SLO compliance looking

00:16:50.740 --> 00:16:53.230
like right now, based on the
metric that you've chosen,

00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:55.630
your window, and your threshold.

00:16:55.630 --> 00:16:58.000
You figure out if
that's what you need,

00:16:58.000 --> 00:16:59.630
if that's the right
target for you.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:02.770
And then you just save the
service, and you're good to go.

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:08.079
So defining an SLO for a
service that you either create

00:17:08.079 --> 00:17:11.619
or define yourself, kind
of different metrics

00:17:11.619 --> 00:17:14.140
that are already there
for you out of the box,

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:16.589
is fairly straightforward.

00:17:16.589 --> 00:17:18.369
In this last part
of the presentation,

00:17:18.369 --> 00:17:19.890
I want to talk
about, what do you

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:22.900
do when the metrics that
you want isn't there.

00:17:22.900 --> 00:17:26.849
How can you go about adding
custom metrics to Google Cloud

00:17:26.849 --> 00:17:31.570
Monitoring in order to use them
as service level indicators?

00:17:31.570 --> 00:17:33.985
I want to discuss
four major options.

00:17:33.985 --> 00:17:36.360
So again, these are metrics
that aren't there for you out

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:37.140
of the box.

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:40.170
So using load balancer metrics
that Mike already talked about

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:41.590
would be a great place to start.

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:43.260
That's an option for you.

00:17:43.260 --> 00:17:45.622
I'm going to talk about
specifically cases where

00:17:45.622 --> 00:17:46.830
the metrics just don't exist.

00:17:46.830 --> 00:17:47.588
What can you do?

00:17:47.588 --> 00:17:49.130
So I'm going to
discuss four options.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:51.450
Log-based metrics,
using the monitoring

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:54.700
API directly, using a custom
instrumentation library,

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:56.790
like OpenCensus
or OpenTelemetry,

00:17:56.790 --> 00:18:00.000
or using Prometheus, which is
a well-known open source time

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:01.880
series monitoring toolkit.

00:18:01.880 --> 00:18:03.660
And as I go down
this list, I'm going

00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:06.198
to talk about of the basics
of each one, how you might

00:18:06.198 --> 00:18:08.490
go about implementing it,
and then talk about some pros

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:10.900
and cons of each so you
can kind of understand,

00:18:10.900 --> 00:18:14.770
what are the trade offs of all
these different approaches.

00:18:14.770 --> 00:18:17.430
So let's start by talking
about log-based metrics.

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:19.440
Fundamentally, log-based
metrics are the idea

00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:23.910
that you can take numerical
values in your logs payloads

00:18:23.910 --> 00:18:26.820
and convert them into metrics.

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:29.890
Out of the box, we ship metrics
that are called system logging

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:31.170
metrics.

00:18:31.170 --> 00:18:33.390
So these are things that
measure, for example,

00:18:33.390 --> 00:18:35.460
log volume ingestion
and other things

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:37.950
so you can understand how
much logging is happening

00:18:37.950 --> 00:18:40.770
within the project and
really how much that's

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:42.402
going to cost you.

00:18:42.402 --> 00:18:47.010
You can then define two
different metric types

00:18:47.010 --> 00:18:49.940
based on your application
of system logs.

00:18:49.940 --> 00:18:52.610
Counter metrics would be
just things that kind of

00:18:52.610 --> 00:18:55.430
tell you how many times
do you see entries that

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:58.580
match the particular filter.

00:18:58.580 --> 00:19:00.290
Distribution metrics
are primarily

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:02.630
what you would use for
SLIs, but you can actually

00:19:02.630 --> 00:19:06.950
use them to extract, using
a regular expression,

00:19:06.950 --> 00:19:10.020
numerical values from
the logs payload.

00:19:10.020 --> 00:19:13.022
So this would be useful for
tracking things like latency.

00:19:13.022 --> 00:19:14.480
So if you don't
have a latency SLI,

00:19:14.480 --> 00:19:17.570
and maybe that latency is being
written in a log somewhere,

00:19:17.570 --> 00:19:20.840
you can use distribution
metrics to do that.

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:23.730
Whereas if you want to do an
availability SLI, where you're

00:19:23.730 --> 00:19:26.630
just counting how many errors,
for example, take place,

00:19:26.630 --> 00:19:28.700
versus number of total
requests, then you

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:31.220
can use a counter metric.

00:19:31.220 --> 00:19:33.620
Obviously the big trade-off
of using log-based metrics

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:37.080
is that this data has
to exist in logs, right?

00:19:37.080 --> 00:19:38.830
So you have to have
logs, and you're going

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:41.430
to be paying for those as well.

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:44.540
And if you already have this
information in your logs,

00:19:44.540 --> 00:19:47.540
this could be a
great place to start.

00:19:47.540 --> 00:19:51.890
You should know that if you're
excluding logs to save on costs

00:19:51.890 --> 00:19:54.920
or save on storage, you can't
configure log-based metrics

00:19:54.920 --> 00:19:57.320
on logs that are being excluded.

00:19:57.320 --> 00:20:01.220
One other thing you should
know is if you want to use SLO

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:04.550
specifically for alerting,
because there can be a delay

00:20:04.550 --> 00:20:08.240
between when logs arrive in the
system and when those values

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:11.180
are essentially aggregated
for the purposes of log-based

00:20:11.180 --> 00:20:14.690
metrics, if you want to
have very precise learning,

00:20:14.690 --> 00:20:16.400
this option may
not actually be--

00:20:19.250 --> 00:20:21.440
the data latency may
not be sufficiently

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:23.510
low for that purpose.

00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:25.640
So when thinking about
log-based metrics,

00:20:25.640 --> 00:20:27.778
the big advantage
of this approach

00:20:27.778 --> 00:20:29.570
is that it really
doesn't require you to do

00:20:29.570 --> 00:20:30.862
any additional instrumentation.

00:20:30.862 --> 00:20:33.740
If logs are already there,
you can just use the data.

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:36.380
The big trade-off is going to
be things like data latency,

00:20:36.380 --> 00:20:38.130
but also you need to
have the logs there.

00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:42.300
And this could result in
additional charges for you.

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:44.310
Your second option for
custom metric telemetry

00:20:44.310 --> 00:20:47.130
instrumentation may be actually
using the monitoring API

00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:51.240
to create the metrics directly
within the monitoring deck.

00:20:51.240 --> 00:20:52.710
So the way you would do that--

00:20:52.710 --> 00:20:55.860
I'm going to walk you through
a basic example of how

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:58.560
you would do that
specifically using Node.js.

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.600
So first you set up
the configuration.

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:02.190
This is where you
kind of set up,

00:21:02.190 --> 00:21:04.580
what is the metric
you want to write.

00:21:04.580 --> 00:21:09.270
It's type, how you'd
actually retrieve it,

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:10.703
and things like that.

00:21:10.703 --> 00:21:12.120
The next step is
you create what's

00:21:12.120 --> 00:21:14.380
called a metric descriptor.

00:21:14.380 --> 00:21:18.240
So this is just kind of--

00:21:18.240 --> 00:21:21.150
it provides the API a way to
determine whether the values

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:22.800
need to be written.

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:25.100
Then you create a
data point object.

00:21:25.100 --> 00:21:29.070
The data point is going to have
an interval that describes when

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:31.300
this information
is being written,

00:21:31.300 --> 00:21:33.930
and then it's going
to have the value.

00:21:33.930 --> 00:21:34.800
What's the number?

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:38.653
What's the time series value
you're actually writing?

00:21:38.653 --> 00:21:40.320
From there you use
the metric descriptor

00:21:40.320 --> 00:21:42.700
to create a time series.

00:21:42.700 --> 00:21:44.370
So remember, in Google
Cloud Monitoring,

00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:46.200
you can have
multiple time series

00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:47.947
against the specific
metric descriptor.

00:21:47.947 --> 00:21:50.280
So this is where you create
that particular time series.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:52.680
And finally, you
actually make a call

00:21:52.680 --> 00:21:57.520
to write your data value against
that particular time series.

00:21:57.520 --> 00:22:00.120
So as you just
saw, there's a lot

00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:05.010
of steps involved if you
want to use the API directly.

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:07.110
The big advantage of
using the Monitoring API

00:22:07.110 --> 00:22:09.750
directly is, because you're
interacting at the lowest

00:22:09.750 --> 00:22:12.270
possible layer here, you have
all the control you need.

00:22:12.270 --> 00:22:15.877
You have full control
over exactly how

00:22:15.877 --> 00:22:17.335
the metric descriptor
gets created,

00:22:17.335 --> 00:22:19.410
how the time series is
directly related, how

00:22:19.410 --> 00:22:21.320
the data points are written.

00:22:21.320 --> 00:22:24.060
So if you need that level
of granular control,

00:22:24.060 --> 00:22:27.750
using the monitoring API is a
great way to accomplish that.

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:30.190
As you saw, the big trade-off
here is complexity, right?

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:33.810
You have to manage all of
these different things.

00:22:33.810 --> 00:22:35.680
So there's probably a
better way to do that.

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:38.670
And what we recommend,
if you actually

00:22:38.670 --> 00:22:40.680
want to do this kind
of instrumentation,

00:22:40.680 --> 00:22:43.650
is to use these custom
instrumentation libraries.

00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:46.080
So you should be aware
that, in the past,

00:22:46.080 --> 00:22:49.570
we've recommended OpenCensus,
which is this open source

00:22:49.570 --> 00:22:52.060
metric and trace
instrumentation library

00:22:52.060 --> 00:22:56.352
that actually came out of the
census library here at Google.

00:22:56.352 --> 00:22:57.810
Recently, there's
been announcement

00:22:57.810 --> 00:23:00.930
that OpenCensus is actually
merging with the OpenTracing

00:23:00.930 --> 00:23:03.995
project in a new project
called OpenTelemetry.

00:23:03.995 --> 00:23:05.370
So I'm going to
show you examples

00:23:05.370 --> 00:23:08.250
of how to do custom metric
instrumentation, both

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:10.200
in OpenCensus, which is
a project that's been

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:12.450
around for a little bit longer.

00:23:12.450 --> 00:23:16.070
And also OpenTelemetry,
which is in your project.

00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:18.040
So we take a look
at a code example

00:23:18.040 --> 00:23:21.930
of how do we use the OpenCensus
metric instrumentation

00:23:21.930 --> 00:23:27.150
library in Node.js to
define a custom metric.

00:23:27.150 --> 00:23:30.390
There are basically a couple
of steps that you have to do.

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:34.380
First, you define a measure,
and then you define a view.

00:23:34.380 --> 00:23:38.100
If you think about it, back to
going to the Monitoring API,

00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:41.970
the view is going to create
the metric descriptor.

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:45.240
The metric is going to create
the specific time series,

00:23:45.240 --> 00:23:47.190
and then you're going
to actually write

00:23:47.190 --> 00:23:49.410
values against those.

00:23:49.410 --> 00:23:51.180
One thing you should
notice is here

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:54.210
at the bottom, where I'm
creating a latency metric,

00:23:54.210 --> 00:23:57.070
because I want to
have a latency SLI,

00:23:57.070 --> 00:23:59.070
I actually have to
explicitly specify

00:23:59.070 --> 00:24:01.250
the buckets of my histogram.

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:03.875
OpenCensus does not allow
you to do that automatically.

00:24:03.875 --> 00:24:05.250
It's very explicit
about the fact

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:07.167
that this is something
that I'm required to do

00:24:07.167 --> 00:24:08.780
as I write my instrumentation.

00:24:08.780 --> 00:24:10.320
So you should be
very cautious when

00:24:10.320 --> 00:24:12.990
you're going to be
tracking latency

00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:15.140
within your application
using OpenCensus.

00:24:15.140 --> 00:24:17.970
You may want to use this
kind of histogram approach.

00:24:17.970 --> 00:24:19.770
When you define the
buckets, you need

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:22.170
to define the buckets in a
way that will actually allow

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:24.836
you to create meaningful SLOs.

00:24:24.836 --> 00:24:26.850
The buckets have to
be precise enough

00:24:26.850 --> 00:24:31.110
to capture the values that
are basically within SLO,

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:34.970
versus those values that
are outside of your SLO.

00:24:34.970 --> 00:24:36.800
As a next step, once
I've got the metric,

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:40.700
I can then go and create
my service level objective.

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:43.130
So this is an example
of an API call

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:47.540
that I would use to specify
the service level objective.

00:24:47.540 --> 00:24:50.360
You can see that I'm
using a request basis SLO,

00:24:50.360 --> 00:24:53.780
and I'm basically saying, I
want to count the requests

00:24:53.780 --> 00:24:57.170
within a specific time interval,
whose response threshold is

00:24:57.170 --> 00:25:01.790
lower than a value, all
across total requests,

00:25:01.790 --> 00:25:03.770
and then I just put a
goal on that SLO, which

00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:06.320
is what percentage of
requests have to fall

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:09.560
within that particular time.

00:25:09.560 --> 00:25:12.800
When I look in cloud
monitoring, there is my metric,

00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:15.880
and then I can
create an SLO again.

00:25:15.880 --> 00:25:20.960
So summarizing OpenCensus,
you have to basically create

00:25:20.960 --> 00:25:22.880
two different parameters.

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:25.480
You create a measure to
define the type of the metric,

00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:27.980
and you have to create the view
to define how that metric is

00:25:27.980 --> 00:25:29.580
going to be added.

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:32.210
And then specifically
for latency SLIs,

00:25:32.210 --> 00:25:35.300
you need to be careful about
how you define your histogram

00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:37.310
buckets to make sure
they're precise enough

00:25:37.310 --> 00:25:39.350
to capture the threshold
that you're going

00:25:39.350 --> 00:25:42.250
to be defining in your SLI.

00:25:42.250 --> 00:25:45.180
Let's now take a
look at an example

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:46.990
of how you do this
with OpenTelemetry,

00:25:46.990 --> 00:25:49.050
which is the new project.

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:51.100
When we take a look
at the code, we'll

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:55.450
see that defining the metric
is quite a bit simple.

00:25:55.450 --> 00:25:58.840
I don't have to go through
the process of creating

00:25:58.840 --> 00:26:00.280
a view and a measure.

00:26:00.280 --> 00:26:04.080
I just simply create the
metric, and there I am.

00:26:04.080 --> 00:26:07.270
All I have to do
is then essentially

00:26:07.270 --> 00:26:09.880
issue a call to
write the balance.

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:13.180
So defining my metrics using
OpenTelemetry is quite a bit

00:26:13.180 --> 00:26:15.790
easier, which is obviously good.

00:26:15.790 --> 00:26:17.620
The big trade-off,
the thing that's

00:26:17.620 --> 00:26:20.650
actually lacking in
OpenTelemetry today,

00:26:20.650 --> 00:26:23.200
is this idea of an exporter.

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:26.550
So in OpenCensus, there's
essentially a native exporter

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:28.780
that essentially allows me
to write metrics directly

00:26:28.780 --> 00:26:30.680
to the cloud
monitoring back-end.

00:26:30.680 --> 00:26:32.860
OpenTelemtry does not
yet have an exporter

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:35.320
for cloud monitoring
for metrics.

00:26:35.320 --> 00:26:38.230
And so I have to essentially
use an intermediary

00:26:38.230 --> 00:26:40.578
to get my metrics
for my application

00:26:40.578 --> 00:26:42.120
into the cloud
monitoring that I can.

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:45.850
In this example, I'm actually
using the Prometheus explorer,

00:26:45.850 --> 00:26:47.410
which will also
load the requirement

00:26:47.410 --> 00:26:49.510
to set up the Prometheus
integration with Cloud

00:26:49.510 --> 00:26:50.470
Monitoring.

00:26:50.470 --> 00:26:52.060
But once I've done
that, I can then

00:26:52.060 --> 00:26:57.010
go ahead and define
my SLO in the same way

00:26:57.010 --> 00:26:59.425
that I did when I
used OpenCensus.

00:26:59.425 --> 00:27:01.300
And you can see, the
metric here actually has

00:27:01.300 --> 00:27:04.480
the external.googlea
pis.com/prometheus prefix

00:27:04.480 --> 00:27:05.160
right?

00:27:05.160 --> 00:27:08.290
Because I'm using the
Prometheus exporter,

00:27:08.290 --> 00:27:10.540
the metrics actually
make it into the back-end

00:27:10.540 --> 00:27:14.260
as Prometheus methods.

00:27:14.260 --> 00:27:16.660
So looking at the differences
between OpenCensus

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:19.690
and OpenTelemetry, OpenTelemetry
doesn't make it easier

00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:20.870
to declare the metrics.

00:27:20.870 --> 00:27:23.150
So the application-side
instrumentation

00:27:23.150 --> 00:27:25.450
is, I think, quite
a bit easier to use.

00:27:25.450 --> 00:27:28.587
The trade-off there is the
ingestion instrumentation

00:27:28.587 --> 00:27:30.670
is a little bit more
complex, because you actually

00:27:30.670 --> 00:27:32.920
need to have an additional
layer of infrastructure

00:27:32.920 --> 00:27:34.270
to do the ingestion.

00:27:34.270 --> 00:27:37.570
And you have to use
Prometheus as an intermediary,

00:27:37.570 --> 00:27:39.850
whereas OpenCensus
allows you to just write

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:41.570
those metrics directly.

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:45.240
So looking at the combination
of OpenCensus and OpenTelemetry,

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:49.420
your big advantage really here
is that you write the metrics

00:27:49.420 --> 00:27:49.960
directly.

00:27:49.960 --> 00:27:52.570
And also, if you want to
get started with distributed

00:27:52.570 --> 00:27:55.240
tracing, you actually
use the same library

00:27:55.240 --> 00:27:58.420
to do both tracing and metrics,
both in the case of OpenCensus

00:27:58.420 --> 00:27:59.905
and OpenTelemetry.

00:27:59.905 --> 00:28:01.780
The trade-offs are going
to be, what language

00:28:01.780 --> 00:28:03.240
is your application written in.

00:28:03.240 --> 00:28:05.270
Does it have support for--

00:28:05.270 --> 00:28:08.140
does OpenTelemetry or OpenCensus
support your application

00:28:08.140 --> 00:28:09.090
language?

00:28:09.090 --> 00:28:11.610
And specifically in the
case of OpenTelemetry,

00:28:11.610 --> 00:28:13.620
how do you get the
metrics into the back-end?

00:28:13.620 --> 00:28:17.740
Are you going to be willing
to manage the Prometheus

00:28:17.740 --> 00:28:20.698
infrastructure to get
that congestion down?

00:28:20.698 --> 00:28:22.990
Which obviously brings us to
our last option, which is,

00:28:22.990 --> 00:28:25.330
well, we can not use
application-level

00:28:25.330 --> 00:28:26.500
instrumentation libraries.

00:28:26.500 --> 00:28:28.990
We can just use
Prometheus directly.

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:32.140
So Prometheus is kind of the de
facto standard for monitoring

00:28:32.140 --> 00:28:34.000
in the open source
ecosystem and certainly

00:28:34.000 --> 00:28:36.460
been something that folks
have been using on Kubernetes

00:28:36.460 --> 00:28:38.030
for quite a while.

00:28:38.030 --> 00:28:40.870
There's lots of migrations
that are available.

00:28:40.870 --> 00:28:42.520
The big trade-off
with Prometheus

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:44.740
is there's essentially
two additional pieces

00:28:44.740 --> 00:28:46.198
of infrastructure
that you actually

00:28:46.198 --> 00:28:48.470
have to maintain in
order for this to happen.

00:28:48.470 --> 00:28:51.110
One is you need the
Prometheus server itself.

00:28:51.110 --> 00:28:53.800
So in the case of
applications running in GKE,

00:28:53.800 --> 00:28:56.360
you need to have the
Prometheus server deployed

00:28:56.360 --> 00:28:58.343
in the same cluster where
your application is.

00:28:58.343 --> 00:29:00.010
And then the second
thing you need to do

00:29:00.010 --> 00:29:02.260
is you need to actually
patch that server with what's

00:29:02.260 --> 00:29:05.955
called the Prometheus
cloud monitoring sidecar.

00:29:05.955 --> 00:29:07.330
The sidecar is
just going to take

00:29:07.330 --> 00:29:09.650
the metrics from
Prometheus and import them

00:29:09.650 --> 00:29:11.330
into cloud monitoring.

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:14.170
So for a specific example of
how this would actually work,

00:29:14.170 --> 00:29:16.060
let's take a look
at the code first.

00:29:16.060 --> 00:29:18.370
You can see that declaring
metrics in Prometheus

00:29:18.370 --> 00:29:22.310
is essentially just as easy
as it was in OpenTelemetry.

00:29:22.310 --> 00:29:23.560
This is a very mature library.

00:29:23.560 --> 00:29:26.890
It's been out for a long time.

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:29.510
There's lots of examples
of how to do this.

00:29:29.510 --> 00:29:32.680
In my example specifically
for latency SLI,

00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:36.760
I again have to manually
or explicitly manage

00:29:36.760 --> 00:29:38.362
my histogram buckets.

00:29:38.362 --> 00:29:39.820
And again, I just
have to make sure

00:29:39.820 --> 00:29:42.730
that I'm setting them up in
a sufficiently precise way

00:29:42.730 --> 00:29:46.990
such that I can then define
SLOs using those methods.

00:29:46.990 --> 00:29:50.630
And here's an example of how
I would write an SLO using

00:29:50.630 --> 00:29:52.700
Prometheus metrics.

00:29:52.700 --> 00:29:57.600
So summarizing that, you can
use request-based SLIs using

00:29:57.600 --> 00:29:58.780
Prometheus metrics--

00:29:58.780 --> 00:30:00.710
pretty straightforward.

00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:02.560
The big trade-off
there is this need

00:30:02.560 --> 00:30:05.980
to maintain additional
infrastructure and a sidecar.

00:30:05.980 --> 00:30:08.380
And specifically, to measure
latency using Prometheus,

00:30:08.380 --> 00:30:10.780
you do need to be
very precise about how

00:30:10.780 --> 00:30:13.130
you define the buckets to
make sure that you have--

00:30:13.130 --> 00:30:15.130
that your buckets are
essentially precise enough

00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:18.130
to capture those
requests that are

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:20.800
within SLO and those
requests are out of the SLO,

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:22.610
at a bare minimum.

00:30:22.610 --> 00:30:25.840
So those are all the
options that we've

00:30:25.840 --> 00:30:28.840
gone through as to how you
might go about instrumenting

00:30:28.840 --> 00:30:31.780
your grown applications
running in Google Cloud

00:30:31.780 --> 00:30:34.990
to expose additional metrics
that you can use for service

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:39.010
level indicators and then
for service level objectives.

00:30:39.010 --> 00:30:42.808
To summarize everything
we've talked about today,

00:30:42.808 --> 00:30:44.350
just like Mike said
at the beginning,

00:30:44.350 --> 00:30:46.660
there is a lot of value
that you can get just

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:48.660
by starting with the
out of the box metrics,

00:30:48.660 --> 00:30:50.740
like load balancer metrics
and things like that,

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:52.405
to define SLIs.

00:30:52.405 --> 00:30:53.863
If you need to
create your own, you

00:30:53.863 --> 00:30:55.385
have to evaluate your options.

00:30:55.385 --> 00:30:57.260
There's going to be
trade-offs with each one,

00:30:57.260 --> 00:30:59.960
so figure out what
makes sense for you.

00:30:59.960 --> 00:31:01.510
But ultimately, it's
really important

00:31:01.510 --> 00:31:03.960
that you focus on user
happiness and monitoring,

00:31:03.960 --> 00:31:06.490
that you really understand
who are our users, how

00:31:06.490 --> 00:31:10.180
do they use our services, how
do our services enable them

00:31:10.180 --> 00:31:13.660
to meet their goals, so that you
can really measure and manage

00:31:13.660 --> 00:31:17.318
reliability while
focusing on the end user.

00:31:17.318 --> 00:31:18.860
Here's what you get
when you do this.

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:20.750
You get SLO dashboards
and cloud monitoring

00:31:20.750 --> 00:31:22.208
that looks like
this, where you get

00:31:22.208 --> 00:31:24.040
all the SLOs in a single place.

00:31:24.040 --> 00:31:26.620
You get any alerts that
are associated with them.

00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:29.120
And as we've covered over
the last few minutes,

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:31.160
the SLOs can be
created on metrics

00:31:31.160 --> 00:31:34.640
that are ingested automatically,
or all the custom metrics

00:31:34.640 --> 00:31:36.200
that we just talked about.

00:31:36.200 --> 00:31:38.360
You also get this
in-context information

00:31:38.360 --> 00:31:40.040
about things like
metrics and logs

00:31:40.040 --> 00:31:41.820
and underlying infrastructure.

00:31:41.820 --> 00:31:46.790
So lots of value that you
get by using this approach

00:31:46.790 --> 00:31:49.100
to monitoring rather
than just relying

00:31:49.100 --> 00:31:51.320
on infrastructure methods.

00:31:51.320 --> 00:31:54.655
So thank you so
much for watching.

00:31:54.655 --> 00:31:56.780
If this is a topic you have
additional interest in,

00:31:56.780 --> 00:31:59.000
here's some other
sessions in this track

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:01.030
that you may want to tune in.

00:32:01.030 --> 00:32:02.940
Really appreciate it.

00:32:02.940 --> 00:32:04.160
Thank you again for watching.

00:32:04.160 --> 00:32:05.390
Mike, thank you so much.

00:32:05.390 --> 00:32:08.410
Really appreciate
you being here.

00:32:08.410 --> 00:32:10.640
And take care, be safe.

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:12.370
Thank you.

